WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
Here in New York City

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.320
lots of big businesses thrive,

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:04.280
but little ones

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.180
struggle.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.720
This is very difficult right now for a small business,

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.840
is very difficult.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.320
Kamal Saleh, who runs this store

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.200
was hit with thousands of dollars in fines.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.560
The first time I think is $2600.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:19.740
His crime?

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.440
He sold three cigars for $8.89 cents.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.500
11 cents too cheap, says New York City.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.420
His customers can't believe it.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:29.200
It's BS.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.320
Kind of stupid

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:31.680
in the sense that

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.580
If somebody's willing to pay something.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:34.820
Not in New York City.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:35.940
Here bureaucrats say

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.680
tobacco must be sold

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.080
for above a minimum floor price.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.120
Why?

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:44.820
Because anti-tobacco crusader Dr. Kurt Ribisl told the

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.760
Center for Disease Control,

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.700
"Very cheap products

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.800
should no longer be available."

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.020
It deters children from starting smoking.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.040
You suggest this at the CDC,

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.235
New York says,

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:58.580
ah good idea!

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.820
Well it's also being considered very seriously

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:02.480
in a number of jurisdictions in California.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:03.800
Why do you get to decide?

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:04.700
No, I'm not deciding.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.320
I'm a person who studies these policies.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.430
I'll let the policymakers decide.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.640
Why do the politicians get to decide?

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.220
Cigarettes are the most lethal product ever introduced.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.226
Thanks to Ribisl's recommendation,

00:01:15.226 --> 00:01:17.700
New York cigarette smokers now must pay

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:18.441
at least

00:01:18.441 --> 00:01:20.140
$13 dollars a pack.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.660
People still have the ability to buy it

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:24.120
if they so choose.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.580
Just not poor people.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.440
We see much higher smoking rates among poor people

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:29.859
and so we need policies

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.440
that are going to reduce

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.220
tobacco use among poor people.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:34.840
I think it's outrageous.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:35.780
I mean first of all,

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:36.940
you're in a poor neighborhood

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:37.760
okay,

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:38.840
and number two,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.200
a lot of people smoke cigars are elderly people,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.120
seniors.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:42.620
You know,

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.520
where you going to charge $3 dollars and more?

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:45.580
They choose it

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:48.000
and the right to make your own choices

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.320
is important.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.920
People are still figuring out ways to afford it.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:52.970
It's not to the point

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:54.720
where no one who's poor is smoking.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.060
Do you know that we have over 20 tobacco retailers

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.520
for every McDonald's in the U.S.?

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:00.700
It must be because

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.200
people want that.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.360
What if you run a tobacco store?

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.180
You're just screwing them.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.840
I want to see a transition

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.249
toward thinking more about healthy food and beverage

00:02:10.249 --> 00:02:11.820
because that's gonna be a future making money.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:12.740
At the CDC,

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.660
Ribisl testified that it should also be

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.040
government policy

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.520
to reduce the number of tobacco stores.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.280
We're not interested in putting

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.740
stores out of business

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:22.920
or making people unemployed.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.440
Sounds like you are.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:26.140
They're gonna find new products to sell.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:27.320
You're a bully.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.220
No, I think the tobacco industry

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.740
is more of a bully.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:32.360
When I say fork it over,

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:33.660
you fork it over.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.020
He referred us to this ad from the FDA.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.125
Cigarettes are bullies.

00:02:38.125 --> 00:02:39.220
They are,

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.380
but New York's smoking police are worse.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:43.385
They use the power of the state

00:02:43.385 --> 00:02:44.420
to get their way

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:45.960
and their tobacco rules

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.800
go on for 47 pages,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:48.560
and this

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.460
is just for tobacco.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.480
For food and refrigeration and

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.360
deliveries and everything else,

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.358
the New York City administrative code

00:02:56.358 --> 00:02:58.077
could be thousands of pages long.

00:02:58.077 --> 00:02:59.131
Lawyer Andrew Tilem

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:01.360
defends store owners who get fined.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.500
You get paid in groceries and pizza?

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.300
Fish

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:05.280
and paper plates

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.160
and tortillas.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.440
The rules mainly hurt little stores.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.880
It's the big guy who basically could hire lawyers.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.040
The big business community is thriving.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.380
You see Starbucks,

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:16.520
you see 7-Eleven,

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:17.439
you see Walmart,

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:18.340
you see Target,

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.500
is the little guy who was trying to, you know

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.294
pinch his pennies and make a dollar

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.100
that has the biggest problem.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.500
Smoking hurts people.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.020
The city says, we're just protecting people.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.720
And I'm not a smoking advocate,

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:30.820
but I think

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:31.730
in this country

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:32.700
and in this city

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.160
that people have the right to do the wrong thing.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:37.240
They ought to.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:38.340
We ought to.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:39.440
But we can't

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:40.480
if the health police

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:41.440
rule our lives.

